                       HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   “Apart, each one answers whoever asks.” She laid her palm on the
Madre’s back. “That’s why they never travel in the same hands. Togeth‐
er they answer each other, and then nobody can make them say any‐
thing else.” She looked at me over the stone. “Yanaisa decides when.
Not you.”
   The gate banged before I could ask what that meant.
   A woman about my age came across the patio in a paramedic’s uni‐
form with the sleeves pushed up and a motorcycle helmet under one
arm. She kissed Abuela on both cheeks, looked at me the way you look
at a package delivered to the wrong apartment, and pulled out a chair
without asking.
   “Yanaisa,” said Abuela. “Tu primo. Juan Carlos.”
   “I know who he is. He’s the one who doesn’t call.” She looked at La
Madre on the table, not at me. “She looks good. Who’s been talking to
her?”
   “Me,” I said. “I think.”
   “You think.”
   “Where’s her son?”
   “Yúcahu is in Villa Mella, under my bed, where he’s been since I
was nine,” said Yanaisa. “He stays there until somebody gives me a
reason.”
   “Abuela says you decide when they meet.”
   “Abuela says a lot of things when she likes somebody.” She stood
and tucked the helmet under her arm. “Nunca las dos juntas. Not in
one bag. Not in one pair of hands. Not yours either, primo. I don’t care
how good your ears are.”
   “I wasn’t going to—”
   “Nobody ever is.” She was already at the gate. “I’m on nights this
week. If you need me, call. If you call and you don’t need me, I’ll
know.”
   The motorcycle started on the second kick and went up the hill com‐
plaining. Abuela watched the gate swing shut.
   “Cabeza dura,” she said, very pleased.




